    "body": "GM just took the wraps off the electric-drive pickup America’s commercial fleets have been waiting for: a one-ton chassis, dually rear axle and massive capability baked in. The best part? You can have it in any color you want — as long as it’s Army green.\nOfficially dubbed the UGV, the newly revealed Unmanned Ground Vehicle is a cab-less, autonomous machine built on what GM is calling, “the dual-rear-wheel Chevy Silverado 3500 HD skateboard architecture,” the UGV is being developed to support military operations with silent (read: electric) logistics and casualty evacuation.\nThe most significant takeaway, however, is that GM has developed an extended-range electric 3500 pickup chassis – and it may be Army-crawling its way to production .\nWishful thinking\nSilverado EV towing; via Kendall Chevrolet.\nGM’s official press release on the UGV concept is understandably short on precise specs, which is the norm for military vehicle announcements. What we can infer , however, has massive potential for the electric truck market. Consider the following reported capabilities: a 102 kWh battery pack, silent electric drive, an onboard internal combustion engine (likely the same 186 hp, 2.8 liter GM Duramax four-cylinder turbo-diesel used in GM’s other Silverado-based military trucks) to recharge the battery and provide continuous V2L and V2G capabilities to equipment in the field.\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nThat’s a ton of capability (sorry) packed into GM’s one-ton dually chassis – and it’s exactly the kind of work-ready EREV or PHEV pickup that America’s work-focused fleets, RVers, and truck enthusiasts have been begging for . And, as for whether or not the UGV really has a chance at seeing production, GM’s own people are hinting that this military concept isn’t a one-off.\n“We do not begin with a blank sheet of paper,” said Steve duMont , president of GM Defense. “By transferring GM’s world-class engineering, supply chain and manufacturing scale directly to the service member, we are transforming customer needs into mission-ready capability faster than ever before.”\nIn addition to the hybrid 3500 chassis, other potential consumer breakthroughs include a more advanced autonomous system than we’ve yet seen in production-spec GM vehicles. The UGV was displayed with Scout AI’s Gx Ground Agent autonomy system, which uses an open, modular interface to accommodate different sensors and mission systems while subtly implying that the eyes-off ADAS scheduled to appear in 2028 under GM’s Super Cruise branding is also making significant progress.\nSo, is GM bringing full-size, body-on-frame, electric drive EREV 3500 dually pickup , with real hot-shot towing capabilities , and eyes-off ADAS to market in 2028 ? A boy can dream, can’t he? Let us know how that all sounds to you, in the comments.\nYOU MIGHT ALSO LIKE :\nHarbinger gears up for war with autonomous military truck program\nAutonomous military 6×6 packs electric drive and go-anywhere grit\nDepartment of War drafts EV battery factory into military drone duty with $75M grant\nIf you’re considering going solar, it’s always a good idea to get quotes from a few installers. To make sure you find a trusted, reliable solar installer near you that offers competitive pricing, check out EnergySage , a free service that makes it easy for you to go solar. It has hundreds of pre-vetted solar installers competing for your business, ensuring you get high-quality solutions and save 20-30% compared to going it alone. Plus, it’s free to use, and you won’t get sales calls until you select an installer and share your phone number with them.\nYour personalized solar quotes are easy to compare online and you’ll get access to unbiased Energy Advisors to help you every step of the way. Get started here .\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on Twitter , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
